23:14:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear your frustration, and I can understand how past experiences—or even the broader narratives we pick up—can leave you bracing for a wall rather than a welcome. If every encounter you’ve witnessed or heard about has felt closed-off, it’s only human to expect more of the same. You’re right that some Muslim communities, like some communities in any faith, can be insular or defensive. Sometimes that’s born of a history of being misunderstood, sometimes it’s poor leadership, and sometimes it’s just a lack of experience with genuine interfaith dialogue. But it's not the whole picture. If you walk in expecting a monologue or a shutdown, it can become a self-fulfilling prophecy—because the defensiveness you anticipate might be noticed on your side, too. What if you gave this particular mosque the chance to surprise you? They’re accepting the invitation, after all. Someone there *wants* to be in that room with you. A practical thought: before the larger group meets, maybe have coffee with their imam or a lay leader. Be honest in a gentle way: “We’ve sometimes found that interfaith conversations can feel guarded. What would make this feel genuinely open and safe for your community, and for ours?” You might uncover shared wariness—and a mutual desire to do it differently this time. It’s also worth remembering that “real openness” doesn’t mean abandoning one’s own truth claims. A Muslim might firmly believe the Qur’an is the final revelation, just as a Christian might firmly believe in Jesus as the only way. Real openness often looks like being able to say, “I see your conviction, I don’t share it, but I want to know you as a person of faith.” That kind of openness is present in many mosques, even if it’s sometimes buried under layers of caution. Invite them, set those shared norms from our earlier chat, and then watch for the individuals in the room, not the monolith. You might just find one or two people who become the most deeply engaged partners you’ve ever had—precisely because they’ve been starved for this kind of honest, respectful space.
